  {
    "@id": "_:g2487500",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicEvent"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/chamberdestroy-thin-edge-new-music-collective-paramirabo"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2487520"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2487540"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/organizer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2487580"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/chamberdestroy-thin-edge-new-music-collective-paramirabo"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "CHAMBERDESTROY / Thin Edge New Music Collective & Paramirabo"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Paramirabo and Thin Edge New Music Collective collaborate on works by Nicole Lizée, Yaz Lancaster, Louis Andriessen, James O'Callaghan and Julius Eastman.ChamberDestroy unites the contemporary music ensembles Paramirabo (Montreal) and Thin Edge New Music Collective - TENMC (Toronto) around a common artistic goal. They celebrate a successful collaboration with the premiere of Darkside by James O'Callaghan &nbsp;and works by Nicole Lizée, Yaz Lancaster's, Louis Andriessen and Julius Eastman.Nicole Lizée, an imaginative Montreal-based composer, fuses contemporary music, technology and popular culture in her works. New York-based transdisciplinary artist Yaz Lancaster explores relational aesthetics and liberatory politics. The experimental style of Louis Andriessen, developed in reaction to post-war serialist traditions, and the haunting harmonic texture of Julius Eastman's Joy Boy enrich this project.Generously supported by The Music Gallery, the Canada Council for the Arts, the Ontario Arts Council and the Toronto Arts Council, this project features custom instruments and graphics that link the worlds of video games and chamber music."
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/startDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2024-11-30T00:30:00-05:00"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g2488100",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicEvent"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/chants-de-passage-andre-pappathomas-grand-choeur-du-centre-sud"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2488120"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2488140"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/organizer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2488180"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/chants-de-passage-andre-pappathomas-grand-choeur-du-centre-sud"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Chants de passage — André Pappathomas — Grand chœur du Centre-Sud "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Conceived and directed by André Pappathomas, Chants de passage is a collective creation in eight movements for choir, four soloists, piano, oboe, Irish bagpipe, tuba, percussion, and invented string instruments.In any form of poetic act, there is a \"passage\" between inspiration and creation... Moments of revelation, as it were. Based on themes written by André Pappathomas, the conception of Chants de passage evolved throughout the rehearsals, creating, through these moments of revelation, unique and touching crossovers between the musical cultures of the soloists and guest instrumentalists.The eight movements of the piece also aim to illustrate, in this first third of our winter, the passage from precious shadow to the essential expression of light."
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/startDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-01-24T00:30:00-05:00"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g2489040",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicEvent"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/musiques-recherches-did-you-say-acousmatic-music-semaine-du-neuf"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/image": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2489060"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/location": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2489080"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/organizer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g2489120"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/concert/2024-25-season/musiques-recherches-did-you-say-acousmatic-music-semaine-du-neuf"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Musiques & Recherches / Did you say acousmatic music ? - Semaine du Neuf"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "A three-hour immersion into the world of acousmatic, immersive and spatialized music in real time, with the result of decades of collaboration between Belgium and Quebec.&nbsp;Composers Julien Guillamat and Annette Vande Gorne present works imagined in the multiphonic studios of Musiques &amp; Recherches in Belgium. In the first part, Annette Vande Gorne, an emblematic figure in acousmatic music, presents&nbsp;Vox Alia, a cycle of five pieces based on the voice, the primary emotional and musical vector, and the ancestral medium of all communication. In the second half, Julien Guillamat performs his latest composition,&nbsp;Altitudes, followed by an extract from his&nbsp;Symphonie de l'étang, an immersive sound work based on the soundscape of the Etang de Thau. Works by David Piazza, Ana Dall'Ara-Majek and Robert Normandeau also testify to the exchanges between Montreal and Musiques et Recherches.With the support of Wallonie Bruxelles InternationalVisual: Luc Beauchemin, “Skrikch Skrakch” (2022-23)"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/startDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-03-09T22:00:00-04:00"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
