    ],
    "http://schema.org/familyName": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "McCausland"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Douglas McCausland is a composer / performer who is fascinated with new sonic territories and processes for creating music, and whose work engages with the extremes of sound and the digital medium. In particular, his current compositions / research explore the intersections of real-time performance of electronic music using handmade interfaces, higher-order ambisonics, interactive systems and performer agency, musical applications of machine-learning, experimental sound design, and DIY electronics / hardware-hacking.\r\n\r\nHis works have been performed internationally at festivals and..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/Person"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5362420",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/torso"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2020"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g5362440"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Torso"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/torso"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Torso&nbsp;is a piece that raises many questions. First of all it is based on material from Charles Ives „the unanswered question“, which I used to come up with the main gestural material of the piece. However it is not necessarily a piece about the Ives example, but rather about the same musical question. The piece makes use of an alternative temporal notation, that goes away from a euclidian space towards a space which is more fluid without and underlying tempo and beat structure.&nbsp;\r\n\r\nThe temporal exposition of the piece is derived from the physical constitution of each player...."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5362460",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/todos-cubierta"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g5362480"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Todos A Cubierta"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/todos-cubierta"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "\"All hands on deck\" the captain cries when his ship is sinking. Everything seems to play against the captain, as the ship won’t stop sinking despite the best efforts of the captain and the crew. The ocean is a remarkable and relentless beast, the ship is old, and you are tired. You are the captain, and you need to sink with your ship.\r\n\r\n¡Todos a Cubierta! depicts scenes of a ship sinking in the middle of the ocean from the point of view of the captain, who is represented by the baritone sax, the rest of the quartet represents the waves, the ocean, the ship and the crew, in different..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5362500",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/lawrence-st"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g5362520"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Lawrence st."
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/lawrence-st"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "lawrence st. is a good place.\r\n\r\nIt’s about waiting for the subway,\r\n\r\nCounting the columns.\r\n\r\nYour eyes are laser beams.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\nlawrence st. is a mathematical sweep,\r\n\r\nConstructed from swirls and grids.\r\n\r\nWhen Pythagoras dies for a bean field,\r\n\r\nDo not be surprised; laugh!\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\nlawrence st. is a meeting,\r\n\r\nChecking in, lightning Kaizen.\r\n\r\nYou have completed your house love.\r\n\r\nSupport each other; breathe.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;- J. Zhu\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5362540",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/mythose"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g5362560"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Mythose"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/mythose"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Mythose is inspired by the cellular reproduction process of a body. The duplication of a sound object leads to a modification of it, at first very slight, then more and more important, until its almost complete deformation. The mother cell is always necessarily present, but it is buried by new versions of itself, acoustic and electronic, which already look almost nothing like it.&nbsp;\r\n\r\n- Chiara Vaneigem\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5362580",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/inter"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g5362600"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "inter-"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/inter"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Written as one of the pieces in a broader project, inter- is a work written for soprano saxophone, baritone saxophone, resonant springs, and ambisonics electronics. In inter-, I’m exploring various modalities and relationships which can be characterized as somehow being “liminal” or falling within an “in-between” state. As a result of this, most of the elements of the piece require the performers to execute materials which are focused on transitions that occur between two points. While I was initially unsure of how this might manifest sonically, the result of this concept yields a..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
