        "@value": "2022"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/composer": [
      {
        "@id": "_:g13077020"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Creation"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/creation-11"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Trodden is a loose and fragmentary setting of Wallace Stevens’ From The Misery of Don Joost, a poem about the difficulty of change, the dissonance between bodily and intellectual knowledge, and the haunting echoes of a haunted life.\r\n\r\nThe main gesture is made up of two dichotomous halves: a quiet, highly ornamental melody, led by the voice, and raucous walls of sound, led by the electric guitar and saxophone. The musicians tread this two-part path many times, slowly adding layers of complexity and difference. As this gesture is repeated – the path trodden-over – the halves bleed into..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
