  {
    "@id": "_:g13488020",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/something-moving-something-still"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2022"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "something moving something still"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/something-moving-something-still"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "something moving something still is a video score in three movements. It makes use of footage and photographs from Millar’s two homes - the neighbourhood where she grew up in Perth, Western Australia and her local Montreal neighbourhood of NDG. Her composition invites musicians to respond to movement and stillness within everyday scenes, bringing attention to daily rhythms and routines of movement, and the spaces around us.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\ncommissioned by SuperMusique\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g13488060",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/le-regard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2022"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Le regard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/le-regard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Le Regard by Laura Kavanaugh is a graphic score composed of images taken from paintings by the artist on three large cotton paper panels. The vibrant colours and geometric shapes are part of a field of vibration in which the music is played. At each rehearsal and at the concert, the instrumentalists play a different image in order to create changes and surprises. Their approach evolves throughout the process and their own sensitivities become a living part of the score.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\ncommissioned by SuperMusique\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g13488100",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/le-fil-dariane"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2022"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Le fil d’Ariane"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/le-fil-dariane"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The inspiration for this composition comes from two famous ARIANES : the mythological heroine (Ariadne) and her famous thread that allowed Theseus to defeat the Minotaur and escape from the labyrinth, and Ariane 5, the satellite-launching rocket that has participated in the construction of an immense labyrinth of millions of satellites, active and deactivated, and of space junk that surrounds our planet. The composition consists of a back and forth between four parts : The crystal ball, The launch, The climbing, The ring of satellites. I conduct the piece, leading the instrumentalists as..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
