    "@id": "_:g15226300",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/o-sacrum-convivium"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "1937"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "O Sacrum Convivium"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/o-sacrum-convivium"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "O Sacrum Convivium was written by Olivier Messiaen in 1937 for four-part mixed chorus. Several iterations of the work came to be, including an optional organ accompaniment and a version for solo soprano and organ, before being officially listed as a piece for mixed chorus a cappella in 1986. Arranged by Lacopo for electric guitar in 2020, this version uses the extended range guitar and electronic processing to re-create the grandiose world of this piece. From octave pitch shifters for added density, chorus modulation for subtle de-tuning effects, and granular delays to replicate..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226340",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/after-dark"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2023"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "After Dark"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/after-dark"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "After Dark is inspired by Haruki Murakami’s novel of the same title. In this story, Murakami builds a surrealist world suspended between fantasy and reality. To replicate the atmosphere of a constantly morphing sleeping city, After Dark presents a conversation between a layer of un-effected guitar and a layer of effected guitar processing through two distinct guitar pedals: the Hologram Electronics Microcosm and the Chase Bliss MOOD. The MOOD is a really special multi-effect guitar pedal; it has two channels that are aware of each other and work together to morph melodic material. After..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226380",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/endless-battle-contrasting-memories"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2023"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An Endless Battle of Contrasting Memories"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/endless-battle-contrasting-memories"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An Endless Battle of Contrasting Memories pulls from two influences. Like After Dark, a quote from Murakami’s 1Q84 initially prompted the writing of this piece: “that’s what the world is, after all: an endless battle of contrasting memories.” Additionally, as used in Frederic Rzewski’s Piano Piece No. 4, An Endless Battle is bookended by an attempt to let the instrument resonate throughout its entire register. 1Q84 suggests a different way of looking at a familiar world. It’s this idea that gives way to the melodic content of the work, used in combination with a loop station to..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226420",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/way-water-breathes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2023"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "the way water breathes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/way-water-breathes"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Lacopo’s approach towards his instrument in the way water breathes pulls from musical influences in progressive music, post-rock, and ambient. Through its fusion with the classical guitar, the piece presents a new way to approach the instrument by incorporating extended techniques typically found in these genres. A new scordatura, often used by the band Covet, transposes the instrument to an open sonority that allows previously impossible gestures to become much more accessible. Alongside the Hologram Electronics Microcosm as a randomized, pitch-shifting delay, and the MOOD as a dense..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226460",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/miniature-beams"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2023"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Miniature Beams"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/miniature-beams"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Vigil is kept at the magnification of tiny twinkles, where augmentation is a rite in this languid journey to the middle of the end. These little glimmers, the sigil of the unfathomable, travel within the emblems of shoulder chips, sleeveless hearts, and tongue tips, granting us the will to accept these symbols as mere reveries. Petite rays scrape the edges off of borders at the strike of each artificial harmonic, offering protection from binaries in structures of 3. Small light seeps through narrow tunnels, but in the absence of containment, inevitably fractures at the site of distorted..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226500",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/buddha-v2"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "1984"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Buddha v.2"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/buddha-v2"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Julius Eastman composed in a style he called \"organic music”. He described this style as undulating, repetitive, and mainly additive. His one-page score, Buddha (1984), allows much freedom. It has twenty stacked staves framed in an egg shape. No bar lines, no set instrumentation, and no audio recording from the composer's lifetime. Emmanuel Lacopo arranged and recorded four versions of the work for the album \"Eastman,\" released by People Places Records in 2023. In Buddha v.1., the notes on the staves are compiled into respective harmonies to form a tape accompaniment, and the electric..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226540",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/when-sun-low"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2021"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "When the Sun is Low"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/when-sun-low"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "When I started working on this piece, Germany had been in a lockdown for more than two months due to the COVID-19 pandemic, and I had barely left the house since the pandemic started in March 2020. On the one hand, like the wintry surroundings I observed through my window, I had reached a state of profound calm. On the other hand, the world had been in upheaval for quite some time. Political polarization, social change, and global warming were causes for concern and demanded a vigilance and willingness to act that had previously been unknown to my generation. When the Sun is Low captures,..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226580",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/phosphorescence"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2023"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Phosphorescence"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/phosphorescence"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Phosphorescence is a process in which energy absorbed by a substance is released relatively slowly in the form of light. The work aims to evoke a feeling of poignant awe when faced with the emittance of an incomprehensible energy source without any perceptible source. Phosphorescence uses the entire register of the 8-string electric guitar through non-traditional technical approaches and incorporates granular delays and looping techniques to mesh the aesthetics of virtuosity and ambient sound worlds.&nbsp;\r\n\r\n— Emmanuel Jacob Lacopo\r\n"
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g15226620",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/song-earth"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2024"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Song of the Earth"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/song-earth"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Like much of {teotwawki}’s music, “Song of the Earth” began with the idea to subvert the electric guitar’s typical dynamic profile. The dominant archetype of the electric guitar is that of a loud instrument, a totem for the rock, metal, punk genres and subgenres it has helped to spawn. The über-decibel doom metal band Sunn O))) is perhaps the terminal point of this logic.&nbsp;\r\n\r\nI decided to explore the other side, the yin to metal’s yang: the quietest possible sounds. An immediate problem arose, the bane of sound engineers everywhere: the “hum” in audio signals resulting..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
