  {
    "@id": "_:g16085560",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/palindrome"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/datePublished": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2024"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Palindrome"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/palindrome"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "**Commissionned by another organisationA palindrome is a sequence that is the same whether it’s read forwards or backwards - a type of mirror pattern. However, a lot of narrative structures are kept together by a palindrome-like symmetry, without being palindromes in the strict sense of the word - a character goes on a journey and then returns, a conflict is created and then resolved, something is lost and needs to be found again, a theme is introduced in the beginning and then reintroduced at the end. Sometimes a story can be described as a ”narrative palindrome”, where in the second..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g16085600",
    "http://schema.org/url": [
      {
        "@id": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/passage"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Passage"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/identifier": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "https://levivier.ca/en/le-vivier/repertoire/passage"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/abstract": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "All over (very fast)Hard edge (fast)Color field (slow, then without tempo)The&nbsp;passage&nbsp;is formally a transitory place or an extract from something larger. You use it to move on to something more important, you don't really stop there, except maybe to make a reminder, to lead to something more elaborate, more meaningful.The history of modernity, and the plastic, historical and pictorial stages which marked out the abstraction process in the 20th century, have always been very significant to me. However, the surface result (the painting, the object) has always been of more relative..."
      }
    ],
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/MusicComposition"
    ]
  },
